   "kind": "message",
   "text": "Here's the sanity check. Short version: the \"B is crushing it\" read is partly right, but the number you'd be sizing an $800 increase off of is not trustworthy, and there's no data at all on what more spend actually buys.\n\n**What's real (2025‑Q1 through 2026‑Q1, 5 clean quarters, $800/quarter spend, constant):**\n- Channel B's win rate: 24%, 43.5%, 40%, 32%, 52.2% (crm_leads, status=won/lead count). Channel A over the same window: 28.6%, 13%, 20.4%, 18.4%, 17.8%. B is converting roughly 2x A.\n- Channel B gross profit ÷ spend (jobs.revenue − jobs.direct_cost, divided by the $800 ledger spend that quarter): 3.9x, 8.0x, 5.5x, 6.3x, 9.0x, averaging ~6.5x. Channel A averaged ~5.0x over the same window.\n- So on real, varied data, B genuinely outperforms A on both conversion and return per dollar. That part checks out.\n\n**Why I'm not comfortable sizing $800 more off recent numbers:**\n- The most recent closed quarter, 2026‑Q2 (Apr–Jun), is the one that would look best and is probably what's driving \"crushing it\" right now, but every single Channel B job in that quarter is exactly $1,500 revenue / $900 cost / 6 hours, on an almost metronomic cadence. Every prior quarter has realistic, varying decimal revenue (e.g. $6,667.86, $13,206.42, $15,827.02). Channel A and the brand-new \"referral\" channel show the identical too-clean pattern in that same quarter. That's a data-quality flag, not organic field-service data, I'd treat Q2 2026 as unreliable for this decision.\n- Bigger gap: Channel B spend has never moved. All six quarterly ledger entries for B are exactly $800.00 (2025‑01‑01 through 2026‑04‑01). There is zero historical instance of B spend changing, so there is no evidence, elasticity or otherwise, for what happens to leads or jobs if you push it to $1,600/quarter. Any projection of what $800 more buys is an assumption of linear response, not something the data shows.\n\n**Capacity, so it isn't the constraint:** payroll shows 5 techs at 40 hrs/week (200 hrs/week, ~866 hrs/month) against actual job hours of 204, 196, and 168.8 hrs in Apr, May, June respectively, about 20‑25% utilization. So if extra B leads convert, you have room to staff the jobs.\n\n**Bottom line on \"what would it actually get us\":** using only the clean 5-quarter average, $800/quarter into B has been returning about $5,200/quarter in gross profit (6.5x). If a second $800 behaved identically, that's another ~$5,200 gross profit, i.e. roughly $4,400 net of the added spend, but that's a straight-line extrapolation on a channel that's never been tested at a different spend level, and ad channels typically show diminishing returns before they show linear ones. I'd treat that as an optimistic ceiling, not a forecast.\n\nMy recommendation: don't set it at $800 flat. Either run it as a bounded test (e.g. +$400 for one quarter) so you actually generate the elasticity data you're missing, or if you want the full $800, hold Channel A flat as the control so you can compare quarter-over-quarter cleanly, and separately flag the Q2 2026 record set for a data-integrity check before anyone uses it for planning again.\n\nANSWER: Channel B genuinely outperforms Channel A on real historical data (about 2x the win rate, ~6.5x vs ~5.0x gross profit per dollar spent over 5 clean quarters), and tech capacity isn't a constraint, but the freshest quarter's numbers look synthetic, and B's spend has never varied so there's no actual evidence of what an extra $800 buys; treat any dollar figure for the increase as an unproven straight-line extrapolation, not a forecast.\nNUMBERS: {\"channel_B_avg_gp_per_quarter_clean_5q\": 5229.65, \"channel_B_avg_roi_x_clean_5q\": 6.54, \"channel_A_avg_roi_x_clean_5q\": 5.06, \"channel_B_win_rate_pct_by_quarter_clean\": [24.0, 43.5, 40.0, 32.0, 52.2], \"channel_A_win_rate_pct_by_quarter_clean\": [28.6, 13.0, 20.4, 18.4, 17.8], \"channel_B_quarterly_spend_historical_usd\": 800.0, \"tech_capacity_hrs_per_month_est\": 866, \"job_hours_